helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148575				0	is	Conditional Brackets				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155312				0	is	You can define a number format so that it only applies when the condition that you specify is met. Conditions are enclosed by square brackets [ ].				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159179				0	is	You can use any combination of numbers and the <, <=, >, >=, = and <> operators.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159196				0	is	For example, if you want to apply different colors to different temperature data, enter:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150872				0	is	[BLUE][<0]#,0 "°C";[RED][>30]#,0 "°C";[BLACK]#,0 "°C"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870				0	is	All temperatures below zero are blue, temperatures between 0 and 30 °C are black, and temperatures higher than 30 °C are red.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154833				0	is	Positive and Negative Numbers				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147295				0	is	To define a number format that adds a different text to a number depending on if the number is positive, negative, or equal to zero, use the following format:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727				0	is	"plus" 0;"minus" 0;"null" 0				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149260				0	is	Percentages and Scientific Notation				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147218				0	is	Prósentur				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151168				0	is	To display numbers as percentages, add the percent sign (%) to the number format.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156005				0	is	Scientific Notation				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3146923				0	is	Scientific notation lets you write very large numbers or very small fractions in a compact form. For example, in scientific notation, 650000 is written as 6.5 x 10^5, and 0.000065 as 6.5 x 10^-5. In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\>, these numbers are written as 6.5E+5 and 6.5E-5, respectively. To create a number format that displays numbers using scientific notation, enter a # or 0, and then one of the following codes E-, E+, e- or e+.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159080				0	is	Number Format Codes of Currency Formats				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147318				0	is	The default currency format for the cells in your spreadsheet is determined by the regional setting of your operating system. If you want, you can apply a custom currency symbol to a cell. For example, enter #,##0.00 € to display 4.50 € (Euros).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150032				0	is	You can also specify the locale setting for the currency by entering the locale code for the country after the symbol. For example, [$€-407] represents Euros in Germany. To view the locale code for a country, select the country in the \<emph\>Language\</emph\> list on the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab of the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog.				20181231 09:50:43
