helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430				0	is	SQL				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152993				0	is	Brella				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155377				0	is	Engin aðgerð				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155533				0	is	No function will be executed.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166420				0	is	Meðaltal				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145268				0	is	AVG				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486				0	is	Calculates the arithmetic mean of a field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149979				0	is	Fjöldi				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260				0	is	COUNT				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155810				0	is	Determines the number of records in the table. Empty fields can either be counted (a) or not (b).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151333				0	is	a) COUNT(*): Passing an asterisk as the argument counts all records in the table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152889				0	is	b) COUNT(column): Passing a field name as an argument counts only fields in which the field name in question contains a value. Null values (empty fields) will not be counted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153067				0	is	Hámark				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148840				0	is	MAX				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159221				0	is	Determines the highest value of a field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146866				0	is	Lágmark				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148604				0	is	MIN				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157982				0	is	Determines the lowest value of a field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154828				0	is	Summa				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147070				0	is	SUM				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154536				0	is	Calculates the sum of values of associated fields.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148820				0	is	Hópur				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145375				0	is	GROUP BY				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149438				0	is	Groups query data according to the field name selected. Functions are executed according to the specified groups. In SQL, this option corresponds to the GROUP BY clause. If a criterion is added, this entry appears in the SQL HAVING.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156038				0	is	You can also enter function calls directly into the SQL statement. The syntax is:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156340				0	is	SELECT FUNCTION(column) FROM table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155075				0	is	For example, the function call in SQL for calculating a sum is:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154591				0	is	SELECT SUM("Price") FROM "Article".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159205				0	is	Except for the \<emph\>Group\</emph\> function, the above functions are so-called Aggregate functions. These are functions that calculate data to create summaries from the results. Additional functions that are not listed in the list box might be also possible. These depend on the specific database system in use and on the current state of the Base driver.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148651				0	is	To use other functions not listed in the list box, you must enter them under \<emph\>Field\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155098				0	is	You can also assign aliases to function calls. If the query is not to be displayed in the column header, enter the desired name under \<emph\>Alias\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155539				0	is	The corresponding function in an SQL statement is:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149425				0	is	SELECT FUNCTION() AS alias FROM table				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144431				0	is	Dæmi:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154614				0	is	SELECT COUNT(*) AS count FROM "Item"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154610				0	is	If you run this function, you cannot insert any additional columns for the query other than receiving these columns as a "Group" function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154644				0	is	\<emph\>Dæmi \</emph\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151120				0	is	In the following example, a query is run through two tables: an "Item" table with the "Item_No" field and a "Suppliers" table with the "Supplier_Name" field. In addition, both tables have a common field name "Supplier_No."				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155144				0	is	The following steps are required to create a query containing all suppliers who deliver more than three items.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153240				0	is	Insert the "Item" and "Suppliers" tables into the query design.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148807				0	is	Link the "Supplier_No" fields of the two tables if there is not already a relation of this type.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3161652				0	is	Double-click the "Item_No" field from the "Item" table. Display the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line using the context menu and select the Count function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151009				0	is	Enter >3 as a criterion and disable the Visible field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145601				0	is	Double-click the "Supplier_Name" field in the "Suppliers" table and choose the Group function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147512				0	is	Run the query.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148638				0	is	If the "price" (for the individual price of an article) and "Supplier_No" (for the supplier of the article) fields exist in the "Item" table, you can obtain the average price of the item that a supplier provides with the following query:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153045				0	is	Insert the "Item" table into the query design.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802				0	is	Double-click the "Price" and "Supplier_No" fields.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153554				0	is	Enable the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line and select the Average function from the "Price" field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155597				0	is	You can also enter "Average" in the line for the alias name (without quotation marks).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151191				0	is	Choose Group for the "Supplier_No" field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155547				0	is	Run the query.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147549				0	is	The following context menu commands and symbols are available:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154172				0	is	Bæta við				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150414				0	is	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides a row for selection of functions.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149872				0	is	Heiti töflu				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147246				0	is	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides the row for the table name.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145117				0	is	Samnefni				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155754				0	is	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides the row for the alias name.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153298				0	is	Aðgreind gildi				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500				0	is	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Applies only distinct values to the query.\</ahelp\> This applies to records containing data that appears several times in the selected fields. If the \<emph\>Distinct Values\</emph\> command is active, you will see only one record in the query (DISTINCT). Otherwise, you will see all records corresponding to the query criteria (ALL).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150436	159			0	is	For example, if the name "Smith" occurs several times in your address database, you can choose the\<emph\> Distinct Values\</emph\> command to specify in the query that the name "Smith" will occur only once.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152352	160			0	is	For a query involving several fields, the combination of values from all fields must be unique so that the result can be formed from a specific record. For example, you have "Smith in Chicago" once in your address book and "Smith in London" twice. With the\<emph\> Distinct Values\</emph\> command, the query will use the two fields "last name" and "city" and return the query result "Smith in Chicago" once and "Smith in London" once.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149825				0	is	In SQL, this command corresponds to the DISTINCT predicate.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148926				0	is	Formulating filter conditions				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153162				0	is	When formulating filter conditions, various operators and commands are available to you. Apart from the relational operators, there are SQL-specific commands that query the content of database fields. If you use these commands in the $[officename] syntax, $[officename] automatically converts these into the corresponding SQL syntax. You can also enter the SQL command directly. The following tables give an overview of the operators and commands:				20181231 09:50:43
