helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3152597				0	is	You can use cells with text to refer to the rows or to the columns that contain the cells.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283				0	is	\<image id=\"img_id3154942\" src=\"res/helpimg/names_as_addressing.png\" width=\"2.1291in\" height=\"0.8709in\" localize=\"true\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3154942\"\>Example spreadsheet\</alt\>\</image\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3154512				0	is	In the example spreadsheet, you can use the string \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Column One'\</item\> in a formula to refer to the cell range \<item type=\"literal\"\>B3\</item\> to \<item type=\"literal\"\>B5\</item\>, or \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Column Two'\</item\> for the cell range \<item type=\"literal\"\>C2\</item\> to \<item type=\"literal\"\>C5\</item\>. You can also use \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Row One'\</item\> for the cell range \<item type=\"literal\"\>B3\</item\> to \<item type=\"literal\"\>D3\</item\>, or \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Row Two'\</item\> for the cell range \<item type=\"literal\"\>B4\</item\> to \<item type=\"literal\"\>D4\</item\>. The result of a formula that uses a cell name, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>SUM('Column One')\</item\>, is 600.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	is	This function is active by default. To turn this function off, choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Calculate\</emph\> and clear the \<emph\>Automatically find column and row labels\</emph\> check box.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3149210				0	is	If you want a name to be automatically recognized by Calc, the name must start with a letter and be composed of alphanumeric characters. If you enter the name in the formula yourself, enclose the name in single quotation marks ('). If a single quotation mark appears in a name, you must enter a backslash in front of the quotation mark, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Harry\\'s Bar'.\</item\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	tit				0	is	Filtering Pivot Tables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150792				0	is	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; filtering tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>filtering;pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150792				0	is	\<variable id=\"datapilot_filtertable\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot_filtertable.xhp\" name=\"Filtering Pivot Tables\"\>Filtering Pivot Tables\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id3153192				0	is	You can use filters to remove unwanted data from a pivot table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id3150441				0	is	Click the \<emph\>Filter\</emph\> button in the sheet to call up the dialog for the filter conditions. Alternatively, call up the context menu of the pivot table and select the \<emph\>Filter\</emph\> command. The \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12090103.xhp\" name=\"Filter\"\>\<emph\>Filter\</emph\>\</link\> dialog appears. Here you can filter the pivot table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id315044199				0	is	You can also click the arrow on a button in the pivot table to show a pop-up window. In this pop-up window, you can edit the visibility settings of the associated field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344485				0	is	The pop-up window displays a list of field members associated with that field. A check box is placed to the left of each field member name. When a field has an alternative display name that differs from its original name, that name is displayed in the list.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344449				0	is	Enable or disable a checkbox to show or hide the associated field member in the pivot table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344493				0	is	Enable or disable the \<emph\>All\</emph\> checkbox to show all or none of the field members.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344431				0	is	Select a field member in the pop-up window and click the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Show only the current item\</item\> button to show only the selected field member. All other field members are hidden in the pivot table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344484				0	is	Select a field member in the pop-up window and click the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Hide only the current item\</item\> button to hide only the selected field member. All other field members are shown in the pivot table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344578				0	is	Commands enable you to sort the field members in ascending order, descending order, or using a custom sort list.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344584				0	is	To edit the custom sort lists, open \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344811				0	is	The arrow to open the pop-up window is normally black. When the field contains one or more hidden field members, the arrow is blue and displays a tiny square at its lower-right corner.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344884				0	is	You can also open the pop-up window by positioning the cell cursor at the button and pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+D.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	tit				0	is	User-defined Number Formats				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143268				0	is	\<bookmark_value\>numbers;user-defined formatting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting; user-defined numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>number formats; millions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>format codes; user-defined number formats\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143268				0	is	\<variable id=\"format_value_userdef\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/format_value_userdef.xhp\" name=\"User-defined Number Formats\"\>User-defined Number Formats\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3150400				0	is	You can define your own number formats to display numbers in \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767				0	is	As an example, to display the number 10,200,000 as 10.2 Million:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868				0	is	Select the cells to which you want to apply a new, user-defined format.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	is	Veldu \<emph\>Sníða - Reitir - Tölustafir\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3149260				0	is	In the \<emph\>Categories\</emph\> list box select "User-defined".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3148646				0	is	In the \<emph\>Format code\</emph\> text box enter the following code:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3152596				0	is	0.0,, "milljón"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3144764				0	is	Smelltu á 'Í lagi'.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3155417				0	is	The following table shows the effects of rounding, thousands delimiters (,), decimal delimiters (.) and the placeholders # and 0.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971				0	is	Númer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3154757				0	is	.#,, "milljón"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3147338				0	is	0.0,, "milljón"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value_userdef.xhp	0	help	par_id3146920				0	is	#,, "milljón"				20181231 09:50:43
