helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145828				0	is	\<bookmark_value\>logical expressions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulating conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>conditions; in fields and sections\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>fields;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sections;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>variables; in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>user data;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>databases;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>hiding; database fields\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145828				0	is	\<variable id=\"defining_conditions\"\>\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090200.xhp\"\>Defining Conditions\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145242				0	is	Conditions are logical expressions that you can use to control the display of \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090000.xhp\"\>fields\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04020000.xhp\"\>sections\</link\> in your document. Although the following examples apply to fields, they also apply to sections.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147171				0	is	You can define conditions for the following field types:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185				0	is	Conditional text: displays text A if the condition is true, or text B if the condition is false.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149289				0	is	Hidden text: hides the contents of the field if the condition is true.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145412				0	is	Hidden paragraph: hides the paragraph if the condition is true.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147515				0	is	Any record and next record: controls the access to database records.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802	18			0	is	The simplest way to define a condition is to type the logical expression directly in a \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box using the following values:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153677				0	is	SATT				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152960				0	is	The condition is always met. You can also enter any value not equal to 0 as the conditional text.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155900				0	is	ÓSATT				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154191				0	is	The condition is not met. You can also enter the value 0.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147090	23			0	is	If you leave the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box empty, the condition is interpreted as not being met.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148980				0	is	When you define a condition, use the same \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>elements\</link\> for defining a formula, namely comparative operators, mathematical and statistical functions, number formats, variables and constants.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153638				0	is	You can use the following types of variables when you define a condition:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155135				0	is	Predefined \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>$[officename] variables\</link\> that use statistics on document properties				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156273				0	is	Custom variables, that are a created with the "Set variable" field				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149174				0	is	Variables based on user data				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145781				0	is	Variables based on the contents of database fields				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155916				0	is	You cannot use internal variables, such as page and chapter numbers, in condition expression.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151375				0	is	Conditions and Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150122				0	is	The following examples use a variable called "x":				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147417				0	is	x == 1 or x EQ 1				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500				0	is	The condition is true if "x" is equal to 1.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145259				0	is	x != 1 or x NEQ 1				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150551				0	is	The condition is true if "x" does not equal 1.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147749				0	is	sinx == 0				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146345				0	is	The condition is true if "x" is a multiple of pi.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149846				0	is	To use comparative operators with strings, the operands must be bounded by double quotation marks:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151078				0	is	x == "ABC" or x EQ "ABC"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150162				0	is	Checks if variable "x" contains (true) the "ABC" string, or not (false).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153301				0	is	x == "" or x EQ ""				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156120				0	is	or				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133				0	is	!x or NOT x				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156112				0	is	Checks if the variable "x" contains an empty string.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150097				0	is	The "equal" comparative operator must be represented by two equal signs (==) in a condition. For example, if you define a variable "x" with the value of 1, you can enter the condition as x==1.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148791				0	is	Notandagögn				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150028				0	is	You can include user data when you define conditions. To change your user data, choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - $[officename] - User data\</emph\>. User data must be entered in the form of strings. You can query the user data with "==" (EQ), "!=" (NEQ), or "!"(NOT).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153124				0	is	The following table lists user data variables and their meanings:				20181231 09:50:43
